# Screen 06 swim around the photographed crest

**Rejected for promotion.** The orange coin arc in the original crosses the cyan crest on its left and then sweeps into a purple opening toward the right. This playable curve moves Benny through that first bend. During the filmed left turn he slips partly off the left side, the purple space inside the crest disappears and the close-set rings still fill the passage. Claudia’s retained route is the version to use.

## What changed in this test

The feature is isolated on `codex/s06-approved-crest-route`. The source commit is `5082930a7e7d7a0dbf914a7b2c4f65376d3984d7`. The baseline is `claude/rainbow-s06-match-c18` at `c649fcbc35780e11cefce83eb54684f1d55752a5`, which was also the Account 17 Screen 06 specialist tip. The exact original has SHA-256 `3946058c0893e97a99dd5015f3197901859475cd7ef55e09d982d232951441fe`. It contains JPEG bytes at 864 × 1536, preserved in APPROVED.jpg.

I traced the contact of each of the 15 photographed coins with the water and fitted a smooth curve in Three.js world coordinates. The actual swimmer, river and foam use that shared course. The fit begins 10.45 units after the inlet and rejoins the original at 48. The start and exit positions and tangents are fixed. Genuine geometric length is 37.54976 units within the original 37.55-unit distance. The largest lateral or vertical combined displacement is 2.263 units. Stage boundaries, water width, steering and speed settings come from the retained project. The far continuation and depth of the bend are interpretations of the single source viewpoint.

Five coins kept their photographed depth. Ten moved closer along their original sight lines, by up to 11.45%. Their physical diameters were scaled in the same ratio to retain their apparent opening sizes. These are the actual pickups. The timing of pickups therefore changed. Both recordings reach the same stage boundary with five hearts. A separate complete ride collected all 91 and reached 2,240 from 1,330.

## What the moving pictures show

- At the original camera the whole-frame RGB comparison is 79.08574% on Claudia’s render and 79.08295% on the test. A pixel counts if its mean absolute RGB difference from the original is at most 20 on the 0–255 scale. Pixelmatch 7.1.0, threshold 0.1 with antialiasing excluded, finds 262,246 and 262,291 differences among 1,327,104 pixels. A fresh retained capture reproduced the original comparison frame pixel for pixel.
- With the original camera held fixed, nine of 81 projected swimmer stations crossed the interior of the photographed purple opening before the change. Zero do in this fit. At +3.75 the projected centre moves from pixel (461, 649) out onto the cyan bend at (245, 677). Across the larger wall mask the count falls from 38 to 17, but the worst remaining projected overlap elsewhere grows from 48 to 74 pixels. These are projected geometry tests. The camera in the actual swim reveals the remaining problem.
- Around 1.0 second the original crest image opens onto a pale area under Benny. From roughly 1.17 to 1.33 seconds the new bend and the real chase camera put much of Benny out of frame on the left. At +3 the river has a clearer left sweep. By +6 the narrow gold rings still form a repetitive stack, and the cyan highlights run into long streaks. The hard-right view still has foam hiding pink fur near the waterline. The opening similarity cannot capture those faults.
- The uninterrupted swim enters Screen 07 at 4.92 seconds. Each of the 63 before and new film frames uses the same course distance, lateral input, shader clock, stage and heart count. The natural chase camera follows the new route. An additional movie and 14 full-size views render the experiment with Claudia’s exact cameras. Different coin collection timing affects the score on 50 film frames.
- All eight existing gameplay checks passed. The dedicated successful full ride kept five hearts, collected 91 coins and finished at 2,240 with no collisions. The Blender inspection file reopens in Blender 5.3.0 with both real river meshes, UVs, 216 centreline stations per version and the original artwork packed into its camera. The shown videos are live Three.js renders from Chromium. Sustained physical Android performance was not measured.

## What would address the remaining gap

Retain Claudia’s gameplay route. The next structural experiment would construct the crest as a curved sheet with a physically open underside, fitted to the two photographed bank edges and the purple wall behind it. Fit the inner drum’s visible rim along the same bend. Confirm that the opening remains visible from the first turn and that Benny stays in frame before extending the hidden parts. The sampled Blender comparison and the failed route are available for that work. No user-approved moving reference exists for the unseen underside yet.

## Reproduction and interpretation

Open `reproduce/queries.json` for Claudia’s retained stage flags and add `cx06CrestRoute=1` for this experiment. Run the project lockfile build in a disposable checkout. The code that fits the world curve is in `tools/crest-route06`. The individual measured camera, input, score and clock records accompany the real images.

Use the instructions in README_FIRST.md for the ready-to-view evidence. For source regeneration, run the Python fitter with the included retained-course.json and the project’s match06 asset directory. Write its fitted route JSON to the experiment asset, then run the Node coin seater from the game root. Its independent numerical continuity check verifies both fixed ends and unchanged samples elsewhere. The fitted optimization reached its evaluation limit, so the exported result is a measured candidate curve rather than a claimed optimum.

Replay the preserved comparisons by setting RAINBOW_BASE_URL to the loopback Vite server and RAINBOW_RUN to a directory that contains the reproduce scripts, flags, fixture and a proof/06/BEFORE folder. Run capture.cjs in the modes stills, ride and full-safe for 06:NEW. The retained camera replay also needs the archived BEFORE poses and route. The matched-moving.cjs and blender-river.py reproduce the common-camera movie and editable mesh inspection. The renderer ran at 60 simulation steps per second, sampled 12 H.264 frames per second at 648 × 1152 and used 864 × 1536 for independent stills. Moving views have no separate approved reference.
